00:19:10.980 --> 00:19:12.340
Like, is it luck based?

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:13.920
Sean, I'm sure you can fill us in here.

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:22.100
Oh, I 100% have been in situations where there is a there's a bad call that the computer has made.

00:19:22.100 --> 00:19:25.840
But then it'll also just decide that I can't challenge.

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:28.100
Like, it's not that it's in the two minute warning.

00:19:28.500 --> 00:19:29.780
It's not that we're in overtime.

00:19:29.780 --> 00:19:32.060
Those are usually situations where you can't challenge.

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.980
But the computer will just say like, no, that wasn't one of the times that we messed up.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:37.000
So you can't challenge.

00:19:37.540 --> 00:19:38.800
And I'm like, but you did.

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:48.840
So I've been very I am also of two minds as to whether there should be computer driven mistakes in like the refereeing of sports games.

00:19:49.060 --> 00:19:55.960
But I will say that presentation wise, it at least does add authenticity, even if it does harm gameplay.

00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:59.860
It's almost like asking why is there wind in golf?

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:01.240
You know, it's like it's a computer.

00:20:01.400 --> 00:20:03.360
It should be able to simulate perfect zero.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:05.200
I think it's different.

00:20:05.200 --> 00:20:09.220
I think it's too different because because that's just part of the game that would be in golf.

00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:14.580
Whereas like somebody making a mistake is like not part of the rules of golf.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.720
It just happens. So you need a way of or, you know, whatever football or whatever.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:19.820
It just happens. So you need a way of combating it.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:24.480
But like if you can remove that and just make it like this is the game under real conditions.

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:26.580
I don't know. I understand, Joe.

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:35.180
It's more like if the computer player laid down another golf ball somewhere else and it was up to you to call him out on the fact that that's not where his golf ball is.

00:20:35.200 --> 00:20:37.140
Well, Landon, that's what would be the equivalent.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:41.040
Right. It would be like Blitz the League, but for golf.

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:42.980
Yeah. Is that what is that?

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.160
What's Blitz the League? Is it like a game where you can cheat?

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:46.160
It's like a game about cheating.

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:49.300
It's a game about cheating in the grand scope.

00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:54.980
Like you could you could get the other team drunk before the before the game the next day.

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:58.500
You could hop yourself up on steroids, all that fun stuff.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:02.900
Yeah. Well, I'm sure in the next MLB The Show, you'll be able to bang a trash can.

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:04.540
So anything's possible.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:10.820
And, you know, since we mentioned Jaleco and Bases Loaded at the beginning of the episode,

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:12.840
did you guys get the vibe on that?

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:19.820
This is the same exact camera angle set up as Bases Loaded had for like the 45 degree overhead.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:23.400
I feel like it's just more intuitive in tennis to have that.

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:26.560
But I couldn't help notice that that's the same way Bases Loaded had it.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.400
And now Racket Attack has it as well.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:30.980
I thought that camera angle was fine.

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:34.980
But I just thought that that was interesting that Jaleco continues to like be like,

00:21:35.060 --> 00:21:36.800
this is the objective way to view sports.

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:49.520
Yeah, I would say that Jaleco has been making surprisingly detailed sprites for the characters with both Bases Loaded and this game.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:59.440
They actually mean, they do just look like they're they're wearing like little jumpers or I forget what rompers rompers.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:04.100
Yes. But other than that, like they are anatomically correct.

00:22:04.100 --> 00:22:07.380
And that's sort of what reminded me of their Bases Loaded game.

00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:11.620
But also one thing I noticed, and this isn't this has nothing to do with baseball game,

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:18.060
but it seems like the the cameraman gets a little woozy at times.

00:22:18.100 --> 00:22:22.400
Have you noticed that the camera is a little weird in this game when it scrolls up and down?

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.780
It kind of loses the ball sometimes.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:30.800
I, you know, I have I have a bigger problem with the camera that I'm sure we're all going to talk about in a second.

00:22:30.980 --> 00:22:41.600
But I did notice, Sean, that just in general, the camera seems to be not necessarily tracking the ball and tracking you as a player.

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:44.160
So I think that could lead to that kind of error.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:47.840
Yeah. You know, they're going for like a handheld look.

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:54.200
Yeah. But to follow up on that, the problem I have really is with the serve.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:57.000
And how it you know, it makes sense.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:23:03.120
Give yourself a different, closer view for the serve so you can line up exactly where you want to hit the ball and all that.

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:08.940
It's the cutaway to the then 45 degree turnover shot.

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:13.160
It's that cutaway while the tennis action is still happening.

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:17.240
You know, it feels like you're just watching TV doing that.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:20.600
But you're but instead, you're the actual player on the field.

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:26.860
So it's almost as if the player on the field is watching the TV feed of the same tennis match that they're playing in.

00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:33.620
Yeah, I think it would have worked a lot better if it means I think this was a technical constraint.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:39.680
But if it was just a straight cut, then we would be probably wouldn't have really cared too much about it.

00:23:39.740 --> 00:23:53.700
But because it basically cuts to black and needs that split second to like re raster the image or like redraw the lines that you kind of there's a separation between the two states.

00:23:53.700 --> 00:23:57.280
So, yeah, I was I was very confused as to why they did that.

00:23:57.420 --> 00:24:06.500
I think maybe they should have just had the same camera angle the whole time, even if it may have cut down on like a like I guess serves are supposed to look different.

00:24:06.720 --> 00:24:09.120
But yeah, I was also confused by that.

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:11.240
Yeah, I didn't like it either.

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:16.560
And I'll be honest, I don't even really get what makes one what makes the serve angle any better.

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:18.200
I don't know why there were two angles.

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:23.880
Maybe someone maybe you guys have a better had a feeling that it was felt a little better to serve in one angle.

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:27.260
But for me, I was just like, why don't they just keep it in the second angle the entire time?

00:24:27.640 --> 00:24:28.480
I couldn't tell you.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:30.160
No, I have no idea.

00:24:30.720 --> 00:24:32.040
No, I think it was.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:42.940
I think it was just from a presentational standpoint that like that's that's how it you know, that was just like a better way to make it look like again, it's all it's all for, you know, the whole thing of bases loaded.

00:24:42.940 --> 00:24:48.640
And now this was to make it feel like you're actually watching a real sporting event.

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:54.020
Right. That's the reason why the only way you can play this game is in an actual full game of tennis.

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:56.940
Right. They're trying to make it as real as possible.

00:24:56.940 --> 00:25:01.000
So that's like their selling point against the arcade style versions of these games.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:09.960
Yeah. But I do agree that this is this is the instance where presentation hurt them because it's disorienting every single time.

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.520
And it never makes you lose.

00:25:11.520 --> 00:25:17.400
Like, it's not like, oh, well, during that cutaway, I actually lost track of the ball and I didn't have time to respond.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:18.380
Like that never happens.

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:31.560
But the fact that every single serve is is at least jarring to me brings down the game, you know, a little bit because it's a constant thing that happens every single, you know, every single time you play this game.

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:32.720
Yeah, I'd agree.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:25:34.640
It's sort of like in a current game.

00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:50.060
If every time you start up, there's like texture, there's like LOD pop in immediately, like every time that would I mean, it may not affect your your gameplay, but it's going to affect your your experience of the game.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:56.920
So I agree. You know, when we were talking about the characters earlier, you guys didn't seem to have a problem with the names of these characters.

00:25:57.760 --> 00:25:59.540
I figured we'd get to it.

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:01.740
Okay. Because, you know, first guy.

00:26:02.140 --> 00:26:05.440
Right. Gipco is a is an interesting fella.

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:09.060
But then the one of the females is a spawn.

00:26:09.080 --> 00:26:11.500
I feel like that that's just weird.

00:26:11.500 --> 00:26:13.420
And I know I don't know.

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:14.580
I'm spawn. Okay.

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:18.440
And maybe I was thinking like, you know, they're probably last names or something like that.

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:25.940
But, you know, that doesn't make it any better for things like spawn or what else did we have?

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:27.700
There was there was another crazy one.

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:30.340
I mean, I just first first first.

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:32.120
First is my big first guy.

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:34.280
He's my favorite.

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:35.600
He's my favorite tennis player.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.760
And one more thing from a design perspective.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:48.200
I really appreciated that the score was kept entirely to the actual layout of the stadium.

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:52.980
So it's just it's just hidden in the background and it's constantly on the screen at all times.

00:26:52.980 --> 00:26:56.580
And it doesn't take up like a chunk of the user interface.

00:26:56.580 --> 00:26:59.580
It's not something you have to hit start to see the score.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:01.340
It's just presented there.

00:27:01.580 --> 00:27:05.260
So minimalistic that I really thought that was smart of them.

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:07.620
But also another surprising stance.

00:27:07.620 --> 00:27:08.940
Guys, thoughts on that?

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:14.400
Well, I think that I actually couldn't find it for the first couple games.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.900
But then I obviously found it.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:26.720
But I was also surprised that we actually have some pretty competent VO for 15 Love.

00:27:26.720 --> 00:27:29.040
You know, all those all those announcements.

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:39.780
It was very clear compared to some of the horror movie versions of human voice that we've heard in the past on this system.

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:47.060
Yeah. I mean, it's funny because it sounds, you know, it sounds like you said a lot better than anything we've heard so far.

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:56.020
Plus, I mean, I've never actually been to a tennis game, but I imagine it sounding like you hear it like a baseball game, which is not that far removed to like this.

00:27:56.160 --> 00:28:00.500
This sound kind of almost unintentionally simulates that like speaker sound.

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:02.060
Kind of the echo. Yeah.

00:28:02.680 --> 00:28:10.080
And it's also got what I can imagine is other than town and country, what in water rage roadshow or whatever it was called.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:16.760
Surf is on. Yeah. This has like this has the first advertisement in a video game, right?

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:17.860
With that Wilson logo.

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.820
Oh, yeah. On the right. At least it was there.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:24.140
Yeah. At least it was like a context.

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:30.520
It was it made sense in the context of a tennis game instead of what you see nowadays.

00:28:30.700 --> 00:28:33.340
But I thought that added to it.

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:40.820
Yeah. I think like apparently in the Japanese version of this, that side of the screen is just blank.

00:28:41.020 --> 00:28:45.760
So they you know, there's no there's nothing that should have been there that was missing.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:47.360
And so they had space to fill.

00:28:47.540 --> 00:28:53.460
That's actually a very well thought out way of filling out the space while also making some money.

00:28:53.720 --> 00:28:54.660
Well, now that you say. Yeah.

00:28:54.740 --> 00:28:56.960
Now I now with Jilco is just sellouts.

00:28:57.140 --> 00:28:59.580
You're not supposed to make any kind of money on video games.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:05.220
Right. But at least it would feel more sellout if they were like advertising something that you wouldn't see at a tennis match.

00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:08.380
Like. So at least it's like, oh, no, we were just going for immersion.

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:10.180
And then Wilson was like, we'll pay you for that.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:15.420
I guess it would make a lot more sense today if it was just like draft kings in the background.

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:19.860
Yes. Or, you know, Jilco could have promoted bases loaded.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:24.200
You know, they could have just put their promotion for their other NES game right in there.

00:29:24.360 --> 00:29:26.420
So I'm sure they did something like that in the manual.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.740
I feel like, you know, that always happens.

00:29:29.340 --> 00:29:31.760
Always. OK.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:43.560
And oh, you know, so I wanted to talk to you guys just about the fact that, you know, we seem to be liking this whole simulation approach and going much different than Nintendo did in terms of recreating tennis.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:52.500
But what do you think about the fact that single player is only this tournament mode and that there isn't like an exhibition mode or a way to choose?

00:29:52.500 --> 00:30:01.080
Like, you know, I just want to play, you know, one set or I just want to play first to three games.

00:30:01.100 --> 00:30:03.080
You know, like what do you guys think about stuff like that?

00:30:03.080 --> 00:30:08.160
Well, I mean, I guess I would just tell that person to only play one set.

00:30:09.560 --> 00:30:16.260
But I know that, like, it's not really adding or removing anything by you're not.

00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.640
I guess you just don't get to see the handshake at the end.

00:30:18.920 --> 00:30:29.640
My knee jerk reaction is to say, yeah, I wish that they gave you the option to play, you know, just play one set and then tournament thing could be another option because that's a long commitment.

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:31.280
But I mean, yeah, that's a great point.

00:30:31.420 --> 00:30:33.300
I mean, you you can just play one set.

00:30:33.300 --> 00:30:42.040
You can stop whenever you want, because at least the two player mode in which it makes more sense to like, I guess, only want to play this for a short burst.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:45.260
It is more of an exhibition style.

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:52.240
But I did find it funny that in the manual it says something along the lines of like in two player, if you want to have a tournament, keep track of it yourself.

00:30:54.600 --> 00:30:58.960
It's like it made it seem like a feature, but it's just like, no, just like write it all down and figure it out.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:04.080
But one other stray nitpick from the manual that I found interesting.

00:31:04.080 --> 00:31:22.460
I don't know if we've ever if it's ever been referred to it this way in other games, because usually when it's like, oh, if you want to play against you want to play against the computer, if you want to play against the AI in this game, they say if you want to play against the control deck, they refer to the computer as the control deck.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:27.460
As if it were some like military technology.

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:30.660
Yeah, it sounds like Skynet is taking over.

00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:33.400
We have to play tennis against it for the fate of humanity.

00:31:34.860 --> 00:31:36.100
I just found that interesting.

00:31:36.700 --> 00:31:45.620
Yeah, I think I think, you know, there's always in these manuals, there's always a different approach to just like explaining how to play video games in general, because that's something that needs to be done.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:58.700
And since you've got me looking at the manual, I'm just wondering, like, the controller layout on on the very first page, like, is that necessary to point out which one's the B button, which one's the A button?

00:31:58.720 --> 00:32:01.360
Like all of those things are labeled on a controller.

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:04.020
The controller is this diagram.

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:12.080
Right. And the control usually on the manuals, the controllers say, like, this button swings the racket and this button pauses the game.

00:32:12.080 --> 00:32:16.880
You know, like for this one to just tell you the same layouts that you see on the NES controller.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:18.000
I don't know. Bizarre.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:24.520
Honestly, I thought that, like, if you were a poor person and you got like the poor person NES, you got an unlabeled controller.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:26.820
So I guess this is just helping those people out.

00:32:26.980 --> 00:32:30.840
The man. You're supposed to cut this out, actually, and lay it over your controller.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:37.220
Yeah. Hey, it's an upgrade from the from the Magnavox Odyssey days.

00:32:37.700 --> 00:32:38.820
Yeah, that's true.

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:51.300
But, you know, Sean, I will agree with your point about, like, if you just want to play one set, I would tell that person to play one set because there are passwords in the game that allow you to resume where you left off.

00:32:51.440 --> 00:32:59.360
So it's not like you're you have to play, you know, a full match in one sitting or anything like that.

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:03.920
Well, you do. You do need to wait until the match is over to get that password.

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:05.600
Gotcha. OK, yeah.

00:33:05.880 --> 00:33:07.660
You only get that while they're shaking hands.

00:33:07.660 --> 00:33:12.420
But is this the first time we've seen any sort of password or saving mechanic in a sports game?

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:13.960
No, it's not.

00:33:14.080 --> 00:33:16.560
What else do we see it in? One of those boxing games.

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:18.240
You could keep your record and everything.

00:33:18.780 --> 00:33:19.700
Oh, I forgot what it was.

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:21.560
Maybe I blocked that out.

00:33:21.840 --> 00:33:23.420
Yeah. Why?

00:33:23.420 --> 00:33:24.620
You hate the boxing games?

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:26.180
The boxing games.

00:33:26.300 --> 00:33:29.180
Yeah. There are a couple of a couple of doozies in there.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:31.560
Yeah, I honestly cannot remember that either.

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:50.660
But I think this this password system of keeping track of your tournament makes complete sense because otherwise you are looking at, you know, a freaking gauntlet of tennis matches back to back to back just to see what is maybe, you know, I don't know if you guys looked it up on YouTube.

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:58.380
But it's just an additional shot of you holding a trophy and your character like seems to kiss the trophy.

00:33:58.680 --> 00:34:01.800
And then and then like it just repeats that animation for a little bit.

00:34:02.120 --> 00:34:06.140
Well, don't forget about the uncomfortably long handshake that you have with your opponent.

00:34:06.340 --> 00:34:07.920
But that happens every time, right?

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:09.300
That happens every time.

00:34:09.480 --> 00:34:11.320
It does. But it's just funny to me.

00:34:12.280 --> 00:34:14.760
I mean, I guess in that case, why play?

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:20.420
Why play any of these games that don't have 15 long 15 minute long cinematics to end the game?

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.640
I mean, like, yeah. What did you think you were going to get?

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:26.120
That's I think I think Final Fantasy VIII is the first good game.

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:31.960
Yeah, I don't think I was going to get anything different, to be honest.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:42.040
I just think it's funny that you might have had to, you know, in a word in a world without passwords, you might have played this whole thing in one sitting to become the champion.

00:34:42.280 --> 00:34:49.700
And then all you see is just like your character holding up the trophy like you would think it would be something a little extra.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:56.920
But Mike, when you get to when you go to school the next day, you could you could tell your lunch table that you beat racket attack.

00:34:57.500 --> 00:35:00.680
And yeah, right. People might respond to that in some way.

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:12.460
Yeah, I don't know. If anybody would even know what you were talking about, because as far as I know, like racket attack is not a game that people talk about, you know, like this is the first time I'm ever hearing about it.

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:14.500
And I'm surprised by it, if we're being honest.

00:35:14.540 --> 00:35:22.260
I'm saying if you were, you know, a kid at a lunch table in 1988, maybe more people would have heard of that about it back then, you know?

00:35:22.260 --> 00:35:27.120
And I'm saying we're fascinated with Jaleco's approach to designing games on the NES.

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:32.120
But they seem to be like they seem to be one of the black sheep of the NES.

00:35:32.220 --> 00:35:33.680
Like no one talks about these games.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:37.300
There's four bases loaded games that I didn't hear about any of them until we played the first one.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:45.520
You're right. Right.

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:51.720
But if you want to tell the kids at the lunch table that you beat racket attack to Joe's point, you can just say that whenever.

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:53.120
No one's going to question it.

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:55.540
But you'll know that it's true.

00:35:55.920 --> 00:36:01.100
Right. You can tell them that, you know, Yoshi was on the top of the castle at the end of racket attack and then they'll believe you.

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:10.660
My only minor thought, which I think we've seen in maybe the other tennis game, is that you can't pause it during during gameplay.

00:36:10.940 --> 00:36:15.360
You can only pause it in between, which I think makes sense because you can't do that in tennis.

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:23.040
But also, I think we should be at a point where, like, you should just be able to pause the video game because that's the point of pausing the game.

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:31.040
Like anything, you know, in Mario, like if you were thinking of that as a real thing, if you're a real guy fighting weird creatures, you wouldn't be able to pause.

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:34.040
But it puts it there for convenience in case something happens in your house.

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:35.420
Yeah. So that's like, you know what I mean?

00:36:35.500 --> 00:36:37.020
I mean, we're not online. So like, might as well put it in.

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:38.640
We're not in an online game.

00:36:38.720 --> 00:36:39.820
Exactly. So you should just be able to pause.

00:36:40.020 --> 00:36:41.940
Yeah. But I thought it was just interesting.

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:45.920
You can pause, like, in between sets or whatever.

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:49.060
But that's my closest to a pause sound I can come up with.

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:53.380
Weird enough, Joe, you actually can't even shut off the game while it's actually playing.

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:57.180
